Recovery reads the journal in runs, not blocks

Journal replay fetched the log through the block cache one 4 KiB block per
device command. On a disk image that is invisible; on a USB stick, where
every command carries a fixed setup cost, it priced a 128 MiB journal at
minutes -- a real drive took over eight of them, DiskArbitration gave up at
twenty seconds (0x3C ETIMEDOUT), and the volume never mounted. A sample of
the wedged extension put the time squarely in jbd_iterate_log ->
jbd_replay_block_tags: one pread per block, through the LUKS layer.

The recovery pass is the one reader that knows its pattern: a strictly
forward sweep of the log area. Give it a read-ahead window that fetches the
journal in physically-contiguous runs (1 MiB at a time, found by walking
the journal inode's mapping) with a single device command each, and serve
replay from that window. The scan and revoke passes keep the per-block
path: they hop between the few header blocks and skip the data between
them, so sequential read-ahead would cost more bytes than it saves in
commands.

The window bypasses the block cache deliberately -- nothing writes journal
blocks between jbd_recover()'s first read and its last, so there is no
fresher copy a cache could hold. Two structural consequences: the
descriptor block being replayed is copied aside before its tags are
walked, because replaying those tags refills the window under it; and
jbd_replay_block_tags() releases its journal-block cache reference through
a single exit path now, which also closes the reference leak the old early
return on a failed superblock write left behind.

Fallback is the old path: if the window cannot be allocated, replay runs
per-block as before -- slow, and correct.

Measured on the incident's shape (128 MiB journal, ~1700 transactions,
LUKS2 underneath): recovery reads fall from 43k device commands to 10k.
Tests/run_replay_speed_tests.sh prices the whole mount like the medium
that produced the incident and holds it inside DiskArbitration's budget.

